# Fidelity Ladder — instance configuration (12-factor).
# The code holds mechanisms; YOUR instance is entirely these variables. Copy to your deploy
# host's EnvironmentFile (see deploy.example/fls-harness.service) — never commit real values.

# ── the surface (GitHub App — OPTIONAL) ────────────────────────────────────────
# Leave this whole section unset and the ladder runs in LOCAL-MODE: `sources.kind=local`,
# `auth.kind=none` (see `GET /system` -> `app: false`). Expeditions live in the local
# ExpeditionStore, admission/climb/kill/wall all work — you just skip the GitHub App
# onboarding (docs/getting-started.md §3 becomes optional). This is the fresh-install default.
#
# To wire the real GitHub surface instead, set all three of these (auto-detected — no extra
# flag needed) and follow docs/getting-started.md §3:
# The repo whose issues ARE your expeditions. Outbound calls refuse when unset (fail-closed).
FLS_REPO=your-org/your-repo
# HMAC secret shared with your GitHub App's webhook. Unsigned/unconfigured -> 403, always.
FLS_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Outbound token (App installation token or a fine-grained PAT: Issues RW + Deployments RW).
GITHUB_TOKEN=
# Explicit overrides (rarely needed — auto-detect from the above is usually right):
# FLS_SOURCE_KIND=github   # or `local`
# FLS_AUTH_KIND=github-app # or `none`

# ── the adjudicator (judges) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Any OpenAI-compatible Azure deployment works; unset -> admission fails closed (needs-human).
AZURE_OPENAI_ENDPOINT=
AZURE_OPENAI_KEY=

# ── builders ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Metered lane (ANCHOR builder.backend: api, or the authorized fallback):
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# Subscription lane (ANCHOR builder.backend: skill-server) — any server speaking
# POST {FLS_SKILL_SERVER}/invoke/{skill} with Bearer auth and {skill, output} replies:
FLS_SKILL_SERVER=
FLS_SKILL_SERVER_KEY=

# ── optional ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A directory containing a playwright npm install, for real rung-3 walkthroughs.
# Unset -> the walkthrough reports unavailable (fail-closed, never a fake pass).
FLS_PLAYWRIGHT_DIR=

# Admin UI build-time override (defaults to same-origin /api — see deploy.example/Caddyfile):
# VITE_FLS_API=

# Local dev tip: bridge your secret store into env with an UNTRACKED script, e.g.
#   scripts/local-env.sh   ->   export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY="$(your-secret-store get ...)"
# The code never reads a credential store directly.

# ── ladder profile + orchestration (harness PoC) ──────────────────────────────
# Which ladder runs. `web-ui` (default, the reference 6-rung ladder) or `harness-poc`
# (rungs 2/3/4 human-gated; Figma frames / design-system prototype / agentic build).
# FLS_PROFILE=harness-poc
# Durable orchestration: unset = in-process climb (default). `temporal` = one Temporal workflow
# per expedition (`fls.orchestration`), sequenced by the worker (`python -m fls.worker`).
# FLS_ORCHESTRATION=temporal
# TEMPORAL_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1:7233
# TEMPORAL_NAMESPACE=default
# TEMPORAL_TASK_QUEUE=fls-harness
# The worker: `stub` proves the plumbing (rung 2 runs one real Claude CLI turn, the rest is
# canned); `live` (later phases) runs the real builders. Threads = concurrent Claude sessions.
# FLS_WORKER_RUNNER=stub
# FLS_WORKER_THREADS=2
# FLS_WORKER_ROOT=/path/to/work        # working directory root for agent sessions
# FLS_MCP_CONFIG=/path/to/mcp.json     # Claude Code --mcp-config (design-tool MCP servers)
# FLS_WORKER_STUB_NO_CLI=1             # stub runner without any CLI call (offline smoke)
# FLS_LIVE_RUNGS=2,3                   # which rungs the live runner really builds (default: 2)
# The design tool file the wireframe rung draws into. Unset -> rung 2 parks (fail-closed).
# FLS_FIGMA_FILE_KEY=
# The design pack the prototype rung composes from: a GENERATED FILE committed next to your app
# (tokens, components, the rules a builder must not break). A file, not a service — so this host
# needs no design-tool credential and every run sees the same system. Unset -> rung 3 parks.
# FLS_DESIGN_PACK=/path/to/your-app/design-system/pack.json
# The vessel the BUILD rung works in: a git checkout of your app on the worker host. The rung makes
# a throwaway worktree on its own branch, so a failed attempt is a deleted directory and the
# checkout everyone else uses is never touched. Unset -> rung 4 parks.
# FLS_VESSEL_REPO=/path/to/your-app
# The project's own check, re-run by the rung after the session finishes. Its exit status is the
# only evidence that counts; "all tests pass" in a final message is not. Unset -> rung 4 parks,
# because a build rung with nothing to verify against is theatre.
# FLS_VESSEL_TEST_CMD=pnpm test:all
# The Claude CLI itself reads its login from $HOME/.claude on the worker host — complete that
# login once, interactively, on the host; the code never touches a credential.

# ── operator identity (the `identity` slot) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WHO may drive this harness. Unset = `none`: every route is open to anyone who can reach the
# URL, including the kill switch and the two endpoints that spend money. That is the right
# default for a laptop and the wrong one for anything reachable.
# Kinds: github-oauth · oidc · proxy-header · none
# FLS_IDENTITY_KIND=github-oauth
#
# The OAuth client. For github-oauth this is an OAuth App (NOT a GitHub App — a GitHub App is an
# installable bot with repo permissions; an OAuth App just answers "who is this person").
# Scope requested is read:user; the access token is discarded once the profile is read.
# FLS_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
# FLS_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
#
# For kind=oidc, the issuer whose discovery document describes the rest. Both
# /.well-known/openid-configuration and /.well-known/oauth-authorization-server are tried, so
# this is the only URL you configure. A hosted provider (Clerk, Auth0, WorkOS, Okta) or a
# self-run broker (Dex, Keycloak, Authentik) federating GitHub/Google/SAML both sit here.
# FLS_OIDC_ISSUER=https://clerk.example.com
#
# ⚠️ REQUIRED, and it must match the provider's registered callback BYTE FOR BYTE.
# It is never derived from the request, for two reasons. The reference Caddyfile proxies with
# `handle_path /api/*`, which STRIPS the prefix — so request.url_for() returns a URL missing
# /api and the provider's exact-match check refuses it. And deriving it from Host/X-Forwarded-*
# turns host-header injection into redirect_uri manipulation.
# FLS_AUTH_REDIRECT_URI=https://admin.ladder.example.com/api/auth/callback
#
# Signs session cookies. REQUIRED — never auto-generated: under `uvicorn --workers N` each
# process would mint a different one, which presents as intermittent 401s that look like a bug
# in the cookie code and are not. 32 random bytes: `openssl rand -base64 32`.
# Accepts several comma-separated keys — the first signs, all verify — so rotating is not a
# forced logout. Append the new key, let sessions drain, then drop the old one.
# FLS_SESSION_SECRET=
# FLS_SESSION_EPOCH=1                  # bump to invalidate every outstanding session at once
# FLS_SESSION_TTL_S=43200
# FLS_COOKIE_SECURE=1                  # 0 only for plain-HTTP local development
#
# Authorization, which the provider does not do for you: a public OAuth app will authenticate
# any stranger on the internet. Neither of these set = fail closed (nobody is admitted).
# FLS_ALLOWED_USERS=octocat,hubot      # logins, emails, or provider subject ids; case-insensitive
                                      # (emails are matched but never written to the ledger)
# FLS_ALLOW_ANY_AUTHENTICATED=0        # for an instance already behind an SSO perimeter
#
# kind=proxy-header only: trust a header from an authenticating reverse proxy (oauth2-proxy,
# Caddy forward_auth). The header is only unforgeable if nothing but the proxy can reach the
# app, so the peer must be listed here or nobody is authenticated.
# FLS_PROXY_USER_HEADER=X-Forwarded-User
# FLS_TRUSTED_PROXIES=127.0.0.1

# ── two variables that were already read but never documented here ──────────────────────────
# The shared passcode for the public visitor surface at /demo/. Unset = the demo refuses every
# login, which is correct: a public surface that authenticates nobody is an open door with a
# form drawn on it. This is a SEPARATE audience from operator identity above, deliberately —
# a visitor session must never satisfy an operator check.
# FLS_DEMO_PASSCODE=
# Cross-origin admin deploys only. Comma-separated, never a wildcard; localhost:8792 is always
# allowed for development. The same-origin /api deploy above needs none of this.
# FLS_CORS_ORIGINS=https://admin.ladder.example.com

# Where a change lands when a reviewer marks its pull request ready for review. Used in the pull
# request's own "what happens when" table, and in the comment the harness leaves after staging.
#
# Deliberately separate from FLS_STAGE_DIR, which is the DIRECTORY the deploy seam writes to.
# One is where the bytes go and the other is where a person goes to look; an instance that
# serves its stage directory on no domain at all is a real configuration, and the pull request
# then says so rather than printing a URL that would 404.
# FLS_STAGE_URL=https://stage.your-app.example.com
